\subsection{Equal periods}{

A stratum gets the same number of units in every period when its sample
size is a multiple of \code{k}. Survey programs that publish estimates by
period allocate in such multiples, for example 24 EAs per district over
12 months. Otherwise the counts differ by one between periods. A stratum
with fewer than \code{k} units is refused, since some period would hold none
of its units and could not estimate it.
}

\subsection{Weights}{

The full-sample \code{.weight} is valid for the combined sample. A period's
own weights come from \code{execute(sample, wave = t)}, which multiplies
\code{.weight} by the exact inverse of the period's activation chance, the
block's quota for that period over the block size. That factor is \code{k}
when a stratum's units fill whole blocks evenly, and it differs from \code{k}
otherwise, so multiplying by \code{k} by hand is not generally valid.
\link[=summary.tbl_sample]{summary()} reports how evenly each stratum fills the
periods.

A period of a sample drawn with equal probabilities exports to survey as a
two-phase design, the activation as its second phase. A period of a
sample drawn with unequal probabilities, such as EAs drawn by size, has
exact weights for totals, but \code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} refuses it
(\code{samplyr_error_wave_phase1_pps}), since survey's two-phase design takes
no unequal probabilities at its first phase. Its variance has no exact
export, and the whole sample's does.
}
